WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.949
Every ticket names an asset,

2
00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:06.325
so the help desk can only be as
right as the inventory it routes to.

3
00:00:06.325 --> 00:00:09.985
The configuration database
earns that trust by agreeing

4
00:00:09.985 --> 00:00:13.112
with one authoritative
identity, not by being one.

5
00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:15.534
Discovery and connectors populate it;

6
00:00:15.534 --> 00:00:18.788
the naming and addressing
record governs identity.

7
00:00:18.788 --> 00:00:23.950
Where the two disagree, the database is
wrong, and the disagreement is a finding.

8
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:28.112
The inventory control asks for no
duplicate accounting of components.

9
00:00:28.112 --> 00:00:32.639
ServiceNow's identification and
reconciliation engine already prevents

10
00:00:32.639 --> 00:00:36.663
duplicates and lets only authorized
sources write each attribute.

11
00:00:36.663 --> 00:00:42.083
The program adds one pass: match each
asset to the naming record first, bind it,

12
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:46.041
and flag the rest, either a
shadow asset nobody approved,

13
00:00:46.041 --> 00:00:50.601
or a gap that's fixed in address
management, never in the database.

14
00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:53.149
Duplicates are resolved by binding,

15
00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:56.462
not deleting, so no open
finding loses its asset.

16
00:00:56.462 --> 00:01:00.112
Stale items are disabled and
attested before they're retired.

17
00:01:00.112 --> 00:01:02.476
It matters because a fix that lands on one

18
00:01:02.476 --> 00:01:05.187
duplicate leaves the
finding open on the other.

19
00:01:05.187 --> 00:01:09.460
And watch the connector that quietly
returns half its usual records.
